			"Message": "refactor(Cocoon): Update import paths to match PascalCase directory hierarchy\n\nFollowing the recent restructuring of the Cocoon source tree into a strict PascalCase directory hierarchy, update all internal import paths to reflect the new file locations. This affects the Bootstrap entry point, Codegen tools, ESBuild configuration modules, Effect services, IPC message handling, and the comprehensive VSCodeAPI handler suite.\n\nSpecifically, update references to split compound filenames (e.g., `TypeConverter.ts` to `Type/Converter.ts`, `PostHogBridge.ts` to `Post/Hog/Bridge.ts`) across the entire module graph. Also remove the deleted `RouteManifest` generated files which are now handled differently. Ensure all relative paths resolve correctly to the new nested directory structure.",
			"SHA": "fbbd8c0ea2db4716f27acaa6d805c4381e2d4c7f"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-04T17:40:07Z",
			"File": [
				"⡏⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				961,
				121
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Cocoon): Standardize file structure with PascalCase directory hierarchy\n\nMigrate the entire Cocoon source tree from flat file naming to a strict PascalCase directory hierarchy, splitting compound names (e.g., `IConfigurationService.ts` becomes `I/Configuration/Service.ts`) into nested folder structures. This affects all core modules including Bootstrap, Codegen, Configuration, Effect, IPC, Interfaces, Services, TypeConverter, and Utility.\n\nThe refactoring enforces the project's naming conventions where every logical segment of a path is a distinct PascalCase directory, ensuring consistent organization across the extension host codebase. All file moves are content-preserving (similarity index 100%), requiring no code logic changes but necessitating updates to import paths throughout the module graph. This restructuring aligns with the architectural pattern established in recent commits regarding ESBuild configuration and service standardization.",
			"SHA": "41d2f5d1d3c4221a1262e9f64e04bc94627ab385"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-04T17:27:24Z",
			"File": [
				"⠃⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				797,
				38
			],
			"Message": "feat(Cocoon): Implement `asWebviewUri` translation for WKWebView assets\n\nReplace the stub `asWebviewUri` implementation in `WindowNamespace.ts` with a functional translator that converts extension-provided `vscode-resource`, `vscode-webview-resource`, and `file` schemes into the `vscode-file://vscode-app` scheme. This adaptation is required because Land's WKWebView environment rejects Service Worker registration on the custom `vscode-webview://` protocol, causing asset loads for popular extensions (e.g., Roo, GitLens) to fail with CORS errors.\n\nThe new logic rewrites URIs to target the native `VscodeFileSchemeHandler` in Mountain, allowing direct disk access for static assets without a service worker round-trip. Additionally, update the `cspSource` property to explicitly include `vscode-file:` alongside legacy schemes to ensure the rewritten assets pass the Content-Security-Policy gate.\n\nRegenerate `RouteManifest.ts` and the full `Target/` build artifacts to reflect the updated service signatures.",
			"SHA": "43335312571896de9b4a9ed127a7642ca5365ff0"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-04T15:09:49Z",
			"File": [
				"⠃⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				797,
				38
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Cocoon): Switch Webview API handlers to named-key payloads\n\nReplace positional argument arrays with named-key object payloads for all Webview RPC calls in `WindowNamespace.ts`. This change ensures that the SkyBridge listeners in the frontend can read parameters like `viewType`, `html`, and `options` directly from the payload without relying on Mountain's positional-to-named canonicalization logic.\n\nThe updates cover `registerCustomEditor`, `unregisterCustomEditor`, `create`, `setOptions`, `setHtml`, `postMessage`, `reveal`, `dispose`, `registerView`, and `unregisterView` methods. While Mountain's gRPC layer preserves the `args` array for legacy compatibility, the explicit named-key structure provides a belt-and-braces approach to prevent parameter misalignment during future Rust-side refactors.\n\nRegenerate `RouteManifest.ts` and all build artifacts in `Target/` to reflect the updated service signatures and current timestamp.",
			"SHA": "e61e0336ecc3476338af94bcf2608317799ce5b4"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-04T11:30:26Z",
			"File": [
				"⠃⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				797,
				35
			],
			"Message": "feat(Cocoon): Extend RPC route manifest with new Webview and Debug handlers\n\nUpdate the generated RouteManifest to include newly registered Mountain-side RPC methods, specifically adding Webview registration/unregistration, raw HTML/message passing, and Debug session controls. This increases the total count of known Mountain methods from 86 to 91.\n\nThe change ensures the Extension Host (Cocoon) correctly recognizes and routes these additional commands defined in the upstream VS Code workbench services to the Rust backend (Mountain). All corresponding build artifacts in the `Target/` directory have been regenerated to reflect the updated method map, ensuring consistency between the service routing logic and the compiled extension host bundle.",
			"SHA": "4f6581102a3e15ce2fc1cd07dcceec23b1f3623f"
		}
	],
